{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_016iGxjDEF764MVAU8n5coqN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"This is very revealing! Let me analyze the key differences between APC 0 and APC 300 in terms of GPU kernel execution:\n\n**APC 0 (top kernels by time):**\n1. `poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes_kernel` — 419ms (17.9%) — 40 instances\n2. `merkle_tree_compress` — 183ms (7.9%) — 160 instances\n3. `merkle_tree_init` — 174ms (7.4%) — 5 instances\n4. `_CT_NTT<8,1>` — 137ms (5.9%) — 80 instances\n\n\n5. `fractional_sumcheck_gkr::compute_round_and_revert_kernel` — 126ms (5.4%) — 238 instances\n6. `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel<false>` — 105ms (4.5%) — 240 instances\n\n**APC 300 (top kernels by time):**\n1. `apc_apply_bus_kernel` — 611ms (20.9%) — 583 instances\n2. `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel<true>` — 374ms (12.8%) — 486 instances\n\nThe shift is dramatic—APC 300 completely dominates with the bus kernel taking over 20% of execution time, while the Poseidon hashing that was the bottleneck in APC 0 has disappeared entirely. The GKR evaluation kernel also becomes significantly more expensive with the `<true>` variant, suggesting a fundamental change in how the proof system is structured between these two configurations. The `<true>` template variants show dramatic increases in execution time and instance counts from APC 0 to APC 300, particularly for `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel` which jumps from 33ms to 374ms, while `calculate_zero_hash` becomes significant only at APC 300. The total AIR instances scale substantially across the two configurations.\n\nWith more APCs, the GPU work fragments into many smaller kernel launches—like the 583 instances of `apc_apply_bus_kernel` averaging just 1ms each—creating excessive launch overhead and poor parallelism utilization since each individual work unit is too small to saturate the GPU. The `calculate_zero_hash` operation, which appears to handle padding for these smaller AIRs, multiplies in frequency as well, with 584 instances each taking around 187μs.\n\nThe real issue is per-AIR overhead: going from 99 to 623 AIR instances means roughly 6x more kernel launches and synchronization points, even though the total work hasn't fundamentally changed. I need to trace through the source code to see where these per-AIR kernels are being dispatched and understand the CPU-side timing structure through the NVTX trace.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8475,"cache_read_input_tokens":48816,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8475},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9fb0c9c1-c32f-45e4-afe7-f108d55d0327","uuid":"66032382-abda-4c14-8247-3a667ac175e3"}
